<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canaletto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1768">1768</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Giovanni Antonio Canal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canaletto, born Giovanni Antonio Canal, was an Italian painter from the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> who painted vedute, views of Venice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, as well as imaginary views called capricci, and was an important etcher. The son of the painter Bernardo Canal, he began as a theatrical scene painter, and much of his early work was painted from nature, with an accuracy that led some to believe he traced it from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camera-obscura">camera obscura</a>, which he owned. Through the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a> he found English buyers, and from 1746 he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, painting London, Westminster Bridge and Warwick Castle. He was elected to the Venetian Academy in 1763 and painted until his death in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canaletto.jpg" alt="Canaletto" width="330" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Visentini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Antonio_Canal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
